<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Landsknecht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1486–17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Landsknechte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Landsknechte were German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> soldiers, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, first raised around 1486 under Maximilian I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> in imitation of the Swiss infantry. They became the chief rivals of Swiss mercenaries and fought for many powers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> and other conflicts of the sixteenth century. They were known for their flamboyant slashed clothing and their own codes of military law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/landsknecht.jpg" alt="Landsknecht" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Hopfer / Erhard Schön / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_German_Soldiers_MET_DP822162.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Landsknecht first appeared in German about 1470 for troops in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles, Duke of Burgundy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> the Swiss pikemen, called Reisläufer, repeatedly defeated Charles and eventually killed him. Archduke Maximilian, who became co-ruler of the Burgundian lands in 1477 by marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a>, was greatly influenced by the Swiss victories. He wanted a permanent force like the Swiss, while the French held a monopoly on hiring Reisläufer. He began raising the first Landsknecht units in 1486, with 6,000 to 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and one unit was trained with Swiss instructors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> in 1487 as the Black Guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1488 Maximilian formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> army of 12,000 infantry and 1,200 cavalry, regarded as the first Landsknecht army raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1490 the Landsknechte helped drive the Hungarians out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, but they refused to serve after sacking Stuhlweissenburg, citing lack of pay. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a> in 1515 they performed well against the Reisläufer. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, with captains such as Georg von Frundsberg, they took part in the victory at Pavia in 1525, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> was captured.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527 was carried out by mutinous, unpaid imperial troops, among them 14,000 Landsknechte under Frundsberg. After his death their reputation declined from the 1560s, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> their bravery and discipline were criticised. The Spanish tercios gained a higher reputation, and increasing indiscipline led to their replacement by the Kaiserlicher Fussknecht, a system less reliant on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Landsknechte were often recruited from South Germany, from a society with growing population and unemployment. Dispossessed craftsmen and peasants served beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, aristocrats and runaway serfs. Their libertine and often brutal way of life was countered by officers who dismounted to fight beside their men and by harsh discipline, including capital punishment. A regiment of about 4,000 men was raised by a lord's letter patent, the Bestallungsbrief, which named the colonel and set out the unit's structure, pay and Articles of War.</p><p>The regiment held ten Fähnlein, each of 400 men including 100 veterans, divided into Rotten of ten ordinary Landsknechte or six Doppelsöldner, the double-pay men who formed the front line with the Zweihänder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a>. A spokesman for the men, the Gemeinweibel, was elected monthly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Landsknecht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its infantry transformed warfare: the success of Swiss pikemen against mounted knights helped end the dominance of heavy cavalry, their tactics were copied by the German <b>Landsknechte</b> and by Spain, and Swiss mercenaries served in most major European armies for three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
